<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      Trinix.Services.Contracts — the native face of every Trinix service, as C#.

      Doc 02 § The two-faces rule: each service has a typed, versioned interface
      that is ours and a compat interface that is somebody else's. This assembly
      is the first of those, and doc 01 makes it the *source of truth* — the D-Bus
      XML is generated from these interfaces rather than the interfaces being
      transcribed from XML. The compat faces go the other way round and are
      hand-written, which is why none of them is here.

      Both ends reference this assembly: the daemon derives from the generated
      handlers, the SDK constructs the generated proxies. That is the whole
      argument for a contracts assembly existing at all — a service whose client
      and server are declared separately is a service whose client and server can
      disagree, and they will.

      Two dependencies, and both are deliberate.

        * Tmds.DBus.Protocol, pinned exactly to 0.95.0. Doc 18 R4 measured it
          under NativeAOT on linux-arm64 against a real dbus-daemon: method call,
          signal, and an `h` file descriptor in both directions including a live
          socket, on both the session and system buses, zero trim warnings, no
          Requires* attributes anywhere, 2.96 MiB. ⚠ Pin *exactly*: seven releases
          in 2026 and a renaming break at 0.93.0 — the type that used to be
          Connection is DBusConnection now.

        * Trinix.Bundle, for the permission vocabulary and nothing else. The same
          reason Trinix.Sandbox takes it: one vocabulary, defined in the assembly
          that already refuses an unknown one at seal time, so that the thing that
          signs a permission, the thing that builds a sandbox from it, and the
          thing that refuses a call for want of it cannot disagree about what a
          permission is.

      ⚠ Tmds.DBus — the high-level package, one dot shorter — is not the one, and
      it will not tell you so. It emits 14+ IL3050/IL2055/IL2060 warnings, then
      fails at ConnectAsync under AOT, and it carries no Requires* annotation, so
      TreatWarningsAsErrors in ../Directory.Build.props is the only thing between a
      developer and that. Sub-trap: it contains its own internal
      Tmds.DBus.Protocol namespace, so a warning naming
      Tmds.DBus.Protocol.MessageReader means the *wrong* package is referenced —
      the real one calls that type Reader and has never had a MessageReader.

      ⚠ This assembly builds with zero trim and AOT warnings — including the
      generated marshallers, `Reader.ReadHandle<SafeFileHandle>()` among them —
      and that claim was checked rather than believed, because a zero-warning
      result from a pipeline nobody has proved *can* warn is not evidence. The
      control: add a file calling JsonSerializer.Serialize((object)x) and build.
      It produces IL2026 and IL3050 here, as errors, from the analysers inherited
      from ../Directory.Build.props. Redo it if this comment ever looks too
      convenient.

      ⚠ What is *not* checked here: ILC. The Roslyn analysers see this assembly's
      own call sites; only a PublishAot on a linux RID sees what the whole graph
      does, and that cannot be run from a Mac. Doc 18 R4 did it for the transport,
      not for this code.
    -->

    <PropertyGroup>
        <RootNamespace>Trinix.Services.Contracts</RootNamespace>
        <AssemblyName>Trinix.Services.Contracts</AssemblyName>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="Tmds.DBus.Protocol" Version="[0.95.0]"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Trinix.Bundle\Trinix.Bundle.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!-- The generator runs here, over the interfaces below, and its output is
             what both the daemon and the SDK end up calling. ReferenceOutputAssembly
             is false because nothing in this assembly calls into the generator; it
             is a compiler plugin, not a library. -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Trinix.Sdk.Generators\Trinix.Sdk.Generators.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
